\section*{Abstract}
Digital accessibility tools for individuals with sensory and speech impairments have historically suffered from architectural fragmentation, rigid modalities, and severe linguistic exclusion, particularly across non-English speaking demographics. Existing commercial assistive technologies typically address isolated challenges—such as text-to-speech, optical character recognition (OCR), or single-turn image captioning—while ignoring the compound needs of individuals facing cross-modal impairments. In this paper, we present \textbf{AgentBridge}, an intelligent, cross-platform, client-edge-cloud assistive system engineered to deliver unified multimodal assistance across visual, auditory, and vocal impairments. AgentBridge introduces a decoupled Master Agent orchestration architecture coordinating specialized perception and conversational capabilities. The system incorporates: (1) an on-device accessibility profiling engine that modulates AI prompt semantics according to explicit user needs; (2) a dual-pipeline multimodal vision framework supporting high-resolution on-demand visual question answering and text reading alongside a sub-second continuous live video narration pipeline over WebSockets featuring strict single-concurrency queueing and multi-model fallback cascades; (3) an on-device multilingual speech processing pipeline handling voice commands and synthesis across English, Hindi, and Telugu with native script and transliteration support; and (4) an ephemeral, zero-persistence memory model preserving user privacy. We conduct an empirical evaluation assessing latency budgets, OCR word error rates, multi-turn conversational robustness, and cross-linguistic command parsing accuracy, benchmarking AgentBridge against leading assistive platforms. Our results demonstrate that AgentBridge delivers end-to-end continuous narration latencies below 850\,ms while providing a scalable, empathetic, and truly inclusive assistive companion.

\noindent \textbf{Keywords:} Assistive Technology, Multimodal AI, Accessibility, Vision-Language Models, Multilingual Speech Recognition, Real-Time Video Streaming, Cross-Disability Systems.

\section{Introduction}

Over 1.3 billion individuals worldwide—approximately 16\% of the global population—experience significant disabilities, with sensory impairments affecting vision, hearing, and vocal expression representing a profound portion of this demographic \citep{who2023disability}. For individuals with visual impairments, navigating physical surroundings, reading ephemeral signage, identifying medications, and recognizing everyday objects require reliable environmental feedback. For deaf and hard-of-hearing individuals, receiving spoken communications and contextual environmental cues necessitates real-time, low-latency textual representations. Similarly, individuals with speech and motor limitations face compounding barriers when forced to interact with voice-first modern digital interfaces that lack augmentative and alternative communication (AAC) mechanisms \citep{bigham2010vizwiz, w3c2023wcag}.

Despite rapid advancements in artificial intelligence and deep neural networks, existing assistive solutions suffer from critical architectural and societal shortcomings:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Modal Fragmentation:} Assistive applications are predominantly single-purpose. Users are forced to switch between disparate apps for OCR document reading, object localization, conversational assistance, and real-time captioning. This cognitive switching penalty severely impacts daily living autonomy \citep{morris2020ai}.
    \item \textbf{Linguistic and Regional Bias:} The vast majority of cutting-edge assistive tools are designed exclusively for high-resource languages (predominantly English). In multicultural and multilingual societies, such as India with over 22 official languages and hundreds of millions of native speakers of Hindi, Telugu, Tamil, and Bengali, visually and vocally impaired individuals face a double penalty: disability compounded by linguistic exclusion \citep{jawahar2021indicnlp}.
    \item \textbf{Latency and Streaming Bottlenecks:} While state-of-the-art multimodal large language models (MLLMs) demonstrate impressive reasoning, naive REST implementations introduce multi-second latencies that are unsuitable for real-time spatial navigation and live environmental narration.
    \item \textbf{Privacy Vulnerabilities in Assistive Feeds:} Traditional cloud-hosted assistive platforms frequently archive continuous user camera feeds, exposing intimate personal living spaces, medical documents, and identifying traits to unauthorized cloud retention.
\end{enumerate}

To overcome these structural limitations, we introduce \textbf{AgentBridge}, an open, extensible, multilingual, and multimodal AI accessibility companion. Designed as an integrated monorepo bridging a lightweight mobile client with a resilient edge orchestration layer, AgentBridge provides seamless cross-disability assistance. 

